{
  "id": "029",
  "title": "The Princess of Asari",
  "subtitle": "\"I was a middle-school music teacher in Brooklyn. I had eight thousand dollars in savings. A man in a navy suit told me I was the heir to a throne I had never heard of.\"",
  "penName": "Nia Okoro-Bellamy",
  "genre": "contractMarriage",
  "hook": "My mother died when I was four. My father disappeared a year later. I was raised by my grandmother in Crown Heights, Brooklyn, in a 3-bedroom on Nostrand Avenue, and I taught middle-school music at PS 191 for seven years. On a Tuesday in October a man in a navy suit came to my classroom between periods. He said his name was Chief Nnamdi Adekunle and he was a minister of Asari, a coastal West African kingdom that had been my mother's home and whose throne had been empty for nineteen years. He said it was mine.",
